      "latency_ms": 2467.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 451,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The water we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which is uniquely *less dense* than liquid water, causing ice to float.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the air as humidity or steam from boiling.\n\nAt standard room temperature and pressure, pure water is **odorless, tasteless, and transparent** (though it can absorb light, giving large bodies of water a blue or greenish tint).\n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nWater is often called the universal solvent because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of the electrical charge of its molecules (it is \"polar\"), water easily bonds with and breaks down salts, sugars, minerals, and gases. This is why oceans are salty and why blood (which is mostly water) can transport nutrients throughout the human body.\n\n### 4. Ecological and Biological Importance\n*   **For the Planet:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans (about 96.5%). It shapes the landscape, regulates the global climate, and drives weather patterns.\n*   **For Life:** All known life depends on water. The human body is made up of about 60% water. It acts as a cushion for organs, regulates body temperature, and serves as the medium for all cellular chemical reactions.\n\nIn short, water is the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
